# Using a Pre-Build Script

Complementary to the post-build hook script, you can also create a pre-build hook script that will be run before the invocation of Maven. This can be used to do some preparations for the build.

The default name of the pre-build script is `prebuild`, which resolves to `prebuild.groovy`, or to the deprecated `prebuild.bsh`. Write hook scripts in Groovy; see [BeanShell scripts](${esc.hash}BeanShell_scripts) for the deprecation of BeanShell.

In a pre-build script you can prepare test resources for your project.

You can also define user properties, which the plugin passes to the build of the test project as `-D` arguments.

Here is an example of a Groovy script (`prebuild.groovy`) that finds a free port and passes it to the build:

```groovy
def userProperties = context.get('userProperties')

def port = new ServerSocket(0).withCloseable { it.localPort }
userProperties.put('serverHost', 'localhost')
userProperties.put('serverPort', String.valueOf(port))
```

Now you can use it in test project:

```xml
<project>
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.example</groupId>
        <artifactId>my-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>@project.version@</version>
        <configuration>
          <server>${serverHost}:${serverPort}</server>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
        ....
       </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
```

# Using a Post-Build Script

Here is an example of how the Invoker Plugin can be used to run a set of Maven projects and then verify their output with a [Groovy](https://groovy-lang.org/) script. The name of the script file in this case is `verify`; the default name is `postbuild`.

```xml
<project>
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <artifactId>maven-invoker-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <debug>true</debug>
          <projectsDirectory>src/it</projectsDirectory>
          <preBuildHookScript>setup</preBuildHookScript>
          <postBuildHookScript>verify</postBuildHookScript>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>integration-test</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>run</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
       </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
```

Below is an example post-build Groovy script (`verify.groovy`) that checks for the existence of a JAR file after the build has run. If the JAR file does not exist, the assertion fails, which causes the Invoker Plugin to log that the build failed. More precisely, any non-null return value which does not equal `true` will be interpreted as a failure condition. And of course, if the script exits abnormally due to an exception, the plugin will flag the corresponding build as a failure, too.

Example `verify.groovy`

```groovy
def file = new File( basedir, "target/my-test-project-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar" )
assert file.isFile()
```

# Writing hook scripts

The plugin runs the pre-build, post-build and selector scripts through the same script runner, but it treats their
results differently. Use the following rules to write scripts that fail with a clear message.

## Return values

A script passes when it returns `null` or a value whose string form is `true`. In Groovy, the value of the last
statement is the return value, so a script that ends with an assignment or a method call can return something you did
not intend. A script that returns any other value fails with a `ScriptReturnException`. A script that throws an
exception or fails an assertion also fails, and the plugin writes the stack trace to the `build.log` file of the test
project.

The effect of a failure depends on the kind of script:

- A failing pre-build script marks the test project as failed in the pre-build hook.
- A failing post-build script marks the test project as failed in the post-build hook.
- A selector script that returns a value other than `null` or `true` skips the test project. A selector script that throws an
  exception ends the test project with an error instead of skipping it.

## Assertions

Use `assert` with a message instead of a `try` and `catch` block that returns `false`. Groovy prints the values of the
expression when the assertion fails, so the message in `build.log` shows what the script found:

```groovy
def report = new File(basedir, "target/report.txt").getText('UTF-8')
assert report.contains("3 modules") : "target/report.txt does not list all modules"
```

## File encoding

Read files with an explicit character set. The `text` property and the `getText()` and `readLines()` methods without an
argument use the platform character set, so the same script can read different content on different machines:

```groovy
def lines = new File(basedir, "target/report.txt").readLines('UTF-8')
def content = new File(basedir, "target/report.txt").getText('UTF-8')
```

## Classpath

Hook scripts run with the Groovy modules that the plugin bundles, plus the dependencies that you add to the plugin
declaration. For the list of modules and how to add more, see [Groovy version and extensions](../groovy-version.html).
Don't rely on libraries such as plexus-utils or Commons IO being on the classpath, because the plugin doesn't
guarantee them. Use the JDK and Groovy APIs, or add the library as a plugin dependency.

## Keep scripts small

Use a script to check behavior that needs a real Maven build, such as the files that a plugin goal creates. Test pure
logic, such as parsing or string handling, in unit tests, where a failure is faster to find and to reproduce.

# BeanShell scripts

Since plugin version 3.11.0, BeanShell (`.bsh`) scripts are deprecated, and support for them will be removed in a
future release. This covers every script the plugin runs: the selector script and the pre-build and post-build hook
scripts, including the indexed ones such as `postbuild.1.bsh`. Write new scripts in Groovy and port existing ones.
Removing BeanShell across Maven is tracked in [apache/maven#13207](https://github.com/apache/maven/issues/13207).

The plugin still runs BeanShell scripts. For each `.bsh` script it runs, it logs a warning once per plugin execution,
both in the Maven output and in the `build.log` of the test project:

```text
[WARNING] BeanShell scripts are deprecated and support for them will be removed, port /path/to/project/target/it/first-it/verify.bsh to Groovy
```

To silence the warning for a while, until the scripts are ported, run with `-Dinvoker.beanShellWarning=false` or
set `<beanShellWarning>false</beanShellWarning>` in the plugin configuration.

To port a script, rename it to `.groovy` and adjust the code. Most BeanShell code is valid Groovy; the `verify.bsh`
below behaves like the `verify.groovy` above:

```java
import java.io.*;

File file = new File( basedir, "target/my-test-project-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar" );
if ( !file.isFile() )
{
    throw new FileNotFoundException( "Could not find generated JAR: " + file );
}
```

Delete the `.bsh` file once the `.groovy` file exists: when you configure a script name without an extension and both
files exist, the plugin runs the `.bsh` one.

A script configured with an extension other than `.bsh` or `.groovy`, such as `verify.txt`, runs as Groovy since plugin
version 3.11.0; earlier versions ran it as BeanShell. The plugin logs a warning for each such script.

# Pre and Post Build Script for multiple project executions

You can run multiple project executions in a single test by defining several `invoker.goals.N` in the `invoker.properties` file.

In this case, the pre and post build hook scripts can be executed for each invocation by adding the index to the script name. For example, if you have two executions, you can have the following scripts:

- `prebuild.groovy` - preprocess before all executions
- `prebuild.1.groovy` - preprocess before the first execution
- `postbuild.1.groovy` - verify the first execution
- `prebuild.2.groovy` - preprocess before the second execution
- `postbuild.2.groovy` - verify the second execution
- `postbuild.groovy` - verify all executions

Missing script files will be ignored.

# Script bytecode level

The pre-/post-build Groovy scripts are compiled to the bytecode level of the project under test, so a project that
sets `maven.compiler.release` or `maven.compiler.target` needs no further configuration. The level is taken from the
first of these that is set:

1. the [`scriptTargetBytecode`](../run-mojo.html#scriptTargetBytecode) parameter,
2. the `maven.compiler.release` property,
3. the `maven.compiler.target` property.

The properties are looked up on the command line (`-D`) first and then in the project. When none of them is set, the
scripts are compiled to the bytecode level of the JDK running Maven. To keep that level in a project that does set
one of the properties, set the parameter to it explicitly:

```xml
<scriptTargetBytecode>${esc.d}{java.specification.version}</scriptTargetBytecode>
```

# Predefined global variables

To allow the scripts to access some useful data about the test project, the Invoker Plugin defines the following global variables before it runs a script. The Scope column shows how often the plugin sets the value:

- Per build: once for the whole plugin execution, the same value for every test project.
- Per job attempt: once each time the plugin runs a test project. The selector script, the pre-build scripts and the post-build scripts of that attempt see the same value. If the plugin reruns the test project, the rerun gets new values.
- Per script run: for each script that runs.

|Name|Type|Description|Scope|Since|
|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|
|`basedir`|`java.io.File`|The absolute path to the base directory of the test project.|Per script run|1.0|
|`scriptdir`|`java.io.File`|The directory that contains the script that runs.|Per script run|3.11.0|
|`localRepositoryPath`|`java.io.File`|The absolute path to the local repository used for the Maven invocation on the test project.|Per build|1.3|
|`context`|`java.util.Map`|The storage of key-value pairs used to pass data between the selector script and the pre-build and post-build hook scripts of one test project.|Per job attempt|1.4|
|`context.get('userProperties')`|`java.util.Properties`|The user properties for executing project|Per job attempt|3.7.0|
|`mavenVersion`|`java.lang.String`|The version of Maven executing on the test project.|Per build|1.9|
|`buildLog`|`java.io.File`|The `build.log` file of the test project, in the log directory if you set `logDirectory`. The value is `null` if you set `noLog`.|Per job attempt|3.11.0|
|`job`|`org.apache.maven.plugins.invoker.ScriptJob`|The test project that runs, with the properties in the following table.|Per job attempt|3.11.0|

The `job` variable has the following read-only properties. In Groovy, read them as `job.basedir`; in BeanShell, call `job.getBasedir()`.

|Property|Type|Description|
|:---|:---|:---|
|`basedir`|`java.nio.file.Path`|The base directory of the test project, the same directory as the `basedir` variable.|
|`buildLog`|`java.nio.file.Path`|The `build.log` file of the test project, the same file as the `buildLog` variable. The value is `null` if you set `noLog`.|
|`localRepository`|`java.nio.file.Path`|The local repository that the test project uses.|
|`mavenVersion`|`java.lang.String`|The version of Maven that runs the test project.|

The `job` variable gives you the paths as `Path` objects, so you can resolve other paths against them. In BeanShell scripts, most method calls on a `Path` object fail on Java 17 and later; use the `File` variables there, or port the script to Groovy.

The plugin sets `basedir`, `scriptdir`, `context`, `buildLog` and `job` for each script, so a [`scriptVariables`](../run-mojo.html#scriptVariables) entry with one of these names has no effect. The plugin logs a warning for each such entry. A `scriptVariables` entry named `localRepositoryPath` or `mavenVersion` replaces the variable of that name, but not the `job` properties.

Additional variables that can be accessed in the hook scripts can be defined through the [`scriptVariables`](../run-mojo.html#scriptVariables) parameter in the Invoker Plugin's configuration.

## Reading the build log

A post-build script often checks the output of the build. Read the `buildLog` variable with an explicit character set:

```groovy
assert buildLog.getText('UTF-8').contains('BUILD SUCCESS')
```

Use `buildLog` instead of building the path yourself: the file isn't in `basedir` when you set `logDirectory`. If you set `noLog`, the plugin writes no build log and `buildLog` is `null`, so don't read the log in that case.
